I observe issue on 28298fa06a 
It seems that it is regression from #22810 
Code below causes setting err to -EALREADY although work is send to queue

```
/* Cancel if work has been submitted */
	if (work->work_q == work_q) {
		err = work_cancel(work);
		/* -EALREADY indicates the work has already completed so this
		 * is likely a recurring work.
		 */
		if (err < 0 && err != -EALREADY) {
			goto done;
		}
	}
```

@pabigot
To clarify what is happening. This is extracted RPA timeout handling which now has a bug that is being ignored because we don't check return value of k_delayed_work_submit in le_set_private_addr.

```
static int le_set_private_addr(u8_t id)
{
	int err = k_delayed_work_submit(&bt_dev.rpa_update, RPA_TIMEOUT);
	BT_WARN("delayed work submit %d", err);
}

static void rpa_timeout(struct k_work *work)
{
	le_set_private_addr(BT_ID_DEFAULT);
}

static int bt_init(void)
{
	k_delayed_work_init(&bt_dev.rpa_update, rpa_timeout);

	le_set_private_addr(BT_ID_DEFAULT);
}

```
Will give:
```
[00:00:04.167,510] <wrn> bt_hci_core: delayed work submit 0
[00:00:19.167,999] <wrn> bt_hci_core: delayed work submit -69
```
So it is not that we changed the API. But that we broke k_delayed_work_submit by doing it.

EDIT:
The problem is that work->work_q is not cleared once the work has finished. So the submit_handler tries to cancel it first.

I think using the function added here in #22826 (we could just have it internal) is the way to solve it.
I cannot see a way to reset work->work_q to NULL. work_timeout would not be the right place it seems.

Tested using this approach and that solves it. Thoughts @pabigot @Vudentz ?

```
diff --git a/kernel/work_q.c b/kernel/work_q.c
index 845a2670c8..fba3f36a34 100644
--- a/kernel/work_q.c
+++ b/kernel/work_q.c
@@ -80,6 +80,12 @@ static int work_cancel(struct k_delayed_work *work)
        return 0;
 }
 
+static inline bool k_delayed_work_pending(struct k_delayed_work *work)
+{
+       return !z_is_inactive_timeout(&work->timeout) ||
+              k_work_pending(&work->work);
+}
+
 int k_delayed_work_submit_to_queue(struct k_work_q *work_q,
                                   struct k_delayed_work *work,
                                   s32_t delay)
@@ -94,7 +100,7 @@ int k_delayed_work_submit_to_queue(struct k_work_q *work_q,
        }
 
        /* Cancel if work has been submitted */
-       if (work->work_q == work_q) {
+       if (k_delayed_work_pending(work)) {
                err = work_cancel(work);
                /* -EALREADY indicates the work has already completed so this
                 * is likely a recurring work.
```

If we want a quick fix something like this might do it @Vudentz since #22810 left the non-zero `err` value to fall through causing the misbehavior:

```
diff --git a/kernel/work_q.c b/kernel/work_q.c
index 845a2670c8..96cd2f644f 100644
--- a/kernel/work_q.c
+++ b/kernel/work_q.c
@@ -99,7 +99,9 @@ int k_delayed_work_submit_to_queue(struct k_work_q *work_q,
                /* -EALREADY indicates the work has already completed so this
                 * is likely a recurring work.
                 */
-               if (err < 0 && err != -EALREADY) {
+               if (err == -EALREADY) {
+                       err = 0;
+               } else if (err < 0) {
                        goto done;
                }
        }
```

But I'm still not really happy about changing the return value of `k_delayed_work_cancel()` from success to error in this case.  We have no idea who else depends on the existing behavior.

